WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.130
- [Judge] Then let us begin with

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:03.120
your opening statement Mr. Payne.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.750
- Whoa, opening statement?

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:07.420
So how can you give an opening statement

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.900
and summarize your case
if you have no idea

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.560
what your case is or
what the case is about?

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:16.283
That's, that's bad news
for Phoenix Wright.

00:00:16.283 --> 00:00:18.866
(upbeat music)

00:00:21.332 --> 00:00:24.280
Hey legal eagles D. James
Stone here teaching you

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.170
how to think like a lawyer.

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:29.360
Today we're covering Phoenix
Wright, Ace Attorney,

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.270
a anime manga based on a video game

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:35.520
that was written in Japanese
and translated into english

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.870
so who knows what we're
gonna to get into today.

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:40.120
My understanding based on your comments

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.960
is that this isn't based on
any particular legal system

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.350
but it's a combination of the
Japanese and American system.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:49.470
I am not an expert in
the Japanese legal system

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:52.060
so I will be looking at
this through the lens

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:55.060
of an American attorney because
that's what I know best.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:57.290
If I get something wrong
please leave me a comment

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:59.540
in the form of an objection.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.760
As the judge and jury in this
case I will either sustain

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.860
or over rule your objection
based on it's legal merit.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:07.930
And as always stick around
until the end of the video

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:10.440
where I give Phoenix Wright Ace Attorney

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.890
a grade for legal realism.

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:15.650
So without further ado let's dig right in

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:17.913
to Phoenix Wright, Ace Attorney.

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:22.330
Defense lawyer Phoenix Wright, okay.

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:23.820
- [Phoenix] I just earned
my badge a few months.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.150
- Hey I think I recognize that area.

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:30.150
So that looks like the Naka
Meguro area of Tokyo along

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:32.770
the Meguro river where they
have all the cherry blossoms.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:34.864
Really pretty area of Tokyo.

00:01:34.864 --> 00:01:36.564
- [Phoenix] So I'm still a rookie.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:39.040
- Did he say he got a badge?

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.240
In the US at least, you don't get a badge.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.320
Police officers get badges.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:45.980
You take the state bar exam

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:47.680
and you become admitted to the bar

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.430
and you don't wear any
sort of lapel pin except

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:53.720
for maybe an American flag lapel pin so...

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.490
don't know what jurisdiction
actually issues a badge,

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:00.780
potentially a gun, to
their defense attorneys.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.363
(upbeat music)

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:07.290
- [Phoenix] In fact
I'm beginning arguments

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:09.453
in my first real trial today.

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:15.930
- So he is on his first day as an attorney

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:17.940
and he's giving an argument in a trial

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.120
that's, that's pretty crazy.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:24.560
Trials take months,
sometimes years to litigate

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:28.160
and you would never start on day one

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:30.020
giving an argument as a new attorney.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:31.950
That's not something
that should ever happen,

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:35.120
if it does something went
terribly, terribly wrong.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:36.880
- Always so ambitious.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:38.710
This is a homicide case,

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:41.870
not many people would take
one on for their first trial.

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:45.740
- Yeah also most defendants
in a homicide case

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:50.410
would not ever use an attorney
who had never done any sort

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:52.550
of trial work before and
it was their first day,

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:56.760
that's definitely not what you
want in a defense attorney.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.197
- I know what I'm
getting myself into boss.

00:02:59.197 --> 00:03:00.030
- Do you?

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:02.320
- But I had to take it
because I owe it to him.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.120
- Oh what's the significance
of the key chain?

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:04.980
- The defendant?

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:06.480
- Yes.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.580
Actually I've known the
guy ever since we were

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:10.590
in grade school, he's part of the reason

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:13.110
I wanted to be a defense
lawyer in the first place.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:14.550
- [Boss] Let's hope
you can clear his name.

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:18.380
- Ah okay so Phoenix Wright
apparently knows the defendant.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:19.900
That can be very problematic

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.670
because you don't want your
emotions to get in the way

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:25.560
of your defense, you have
certain ethical obligations

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:30.560
to your client and if your
emotions get in the way of that

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:34.190
and also if you're a
witness who might testify

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:36.210
to the character of that defendant

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:39.190
then you would mainly be conflicted out

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:41.940
of representing them so
there's a lot of issues here

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:44.210
representing someone who
is a childhood friend,

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:48.150
I would never recommend for
someone to represent someone

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:50.900
that they know so well,
that's, that's not a good idea.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:55.560
- There's no way he's guilty

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:57.513
but how will I convince the judge?

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.073
- Do you not have any
evidence at this point?

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.050
Have you not spoken to your client?

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:07.140
Okay and they're separating people.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:09.610
So this is a very ornate court room

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:12.720
that would be more suited
to an appeals court,

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.543
not a trial court at the criminal level.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:23.400
Winston Payne, the prosecutor.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:25.940
- [Phoenix] That's my
client, good old Larry Butz.

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:26.893
- Okay that's an interesting name.

00:04:26.893 --> 00:04:28.357
- [Phoenix] He and I go
way back, to say the least.

00:04:28.357 --> 00:04:29.873
And you've been dreaming of.

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:33.390
- And he's apparently
wearing the same thing

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:36.563
that he was wearing on
the day of the murder.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:40.550
You wanna put your client
in the best light forward

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:43.590
so that's why defense
attorneys, if they have to,

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:46.700
will provide a suit and
tie to their clients

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:49.690
in order to make them look a little better

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:51.790
than if they'd just
walked in off the street.

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:55.170
You would never want
to wear just a t-shirt

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:58.200
with some crazy hair
and a goatee to court,

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.740
you'd want to dress them up a little bit

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:02.940
so they don't look like a hooligan

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:06.600
like this guy kinda looks
like a frat guy or something.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:08.760
- Don't forget, to argue a solid defense

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:11.720
you have to believe in
him, and his innocence.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:12.980
- Right.

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:15.180
- Not necessarily.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:19.070
All clients are owed an advocate

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:21.460
and often you are conflicted

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:23.580
because you may see the evidence

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:25.500
and you may believe they are guilty

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.740
or libel of what they're accused of.

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:30.670
I have heard from my
public defender friends,

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:34.150
those people who provide
defenses to the indigent

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:35.740
that it can actually be harder

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:38.030
if you truly believe
that someone is innocent.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:42.530
If you aren't sure and you're
just giving them a defense

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:43.780
you can go through the motions

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:45.930
and you can give them the
best defense possible.

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:48.340
But if you really believe
that they're innocent

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:52.020
of a particular crime then
it can actually be harder

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:56.030
because you are worried
that they may go to jail

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:59.010
or be convicted of something
that they didn't do

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:01.717
which can actually make it
harder for public defenders.

00:06:01.717 --> 00:06:04.467
(dramatic music)

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:09.240
The judge.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:11.630
- This court is here by in session.

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:12.463
- Okay.

00:06:12.463 --> 00:06:13.920
- We have been gathered
today to hear the case

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:15.670
against Mr. Larry Butz.

00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:17.597
- The prosecution is ready your honor.

00:06:17.597 --> 00:06:18.430
- All right.

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:20.130
- The defense is ready your honor.

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:22.970
- Then let us begin with your
opening statement Mr. Payne.

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:24.640
- Whoa, opening statement?

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:29.330
So (laughs) the opening statement is

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:31.760
what you give at the beginning of a trial.

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:34.590
And it seems like there
has been very little time

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:37.420
that has passed between
the day of the murder

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:39.630
and this day in court.

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:42.110
Larry Butz is apparently
wearing the exact same thing

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:47.070
that he was wearing on
the day of the murder

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:48.450
and it doesn't seem like Phoenix Wright

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:51.110
has actually spoken to his client yet.

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:55.060
However, at the bare minimum you've gotta

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:55.893
talk to your client,

00:06:55.893 --> 00:06:57.270
you've gotta talk to them,

00:06:57.270 --> 00:06:59.830
you've gotta do some
investigation about the facts,

00:06:59.830 --> 00:07:01.563
you've gotta conduct discovery.

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:05.610
The prosecution is obligated
to turn over the evidence

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:06.940
that they have to the defense

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:10.260
and you are obligated to
review that information

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:12.070
before the trial.

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:15.490
That he hasn't apparently
spoken to his client yet

00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:16.580
and it's the day of trial

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:17.950
and he has to give an opening statement,

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:19.670
that's crazy how can you
give an opening statement

00:07:19.670 --> 00:07:22.160
and summarize your case
if you have no idea

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:24.820
what your case is or
what the case is about?

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:28.613
That's, that's bad news
for Phoenix Wright.

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:31.110
- [Payne] We've identified
this statue out of

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:33.230
Rodin's the Thinker as the murder weapon.

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:34.063
- Alright.

00:07:34.063 --> 00:07:36.620
- Which I would like to submit
to the court at this time.

00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:40.660
- Generally exhibits would be determined

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:42.220
before the trial goes forward.

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:44.000
- [Judge] a modern representation.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:45.930
- Didn't you just hear the lawyer guy?

00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:47.980
It's Rodin, not Mo Dern,

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:50.220
and this was a gift I
made for my girlfriend.

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:52.250
- Do you mean you sculpted this.

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:53.500
- And why is he speaking?

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:55.890
This is opening statements,

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:58.080
the attorneys make an argument

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:00.670
or they say what the
evidence is going to show

00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:02.040
for the rest of the trial,

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:04.170
this is not the time for
the criminal defendant

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:05.460
to say anything at all,

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:07.470
especially things that are inculpatory,

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:09.890
that's you need to get your client

00:08:09.890 --> 00:08:11.800
to shut the hell up at this point.

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:13.740
- [Payne] Fingerprints
were indeed all over

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:14.930
the weapon your honor.

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:15.943
- Are you serious?

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:18.590
- Shut up, shut up.

00:08:18.590 --> 00:08:20.210
- Your prints would be
all over it you idiot.

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:21.180
- In general, that's good advice

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:22.467
just tell you client to shut up.

00:08:22.467 --> 00:08:25.813
- Let me state my case
man, I can't hang for this.

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:29.660
- The prosecution would
not like to call Mr. Butz

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:30.900
to the stand your honor.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:34.820
- Is Phoenix Wright not get
to make an opening statement?

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:38.140
If trial has started then
prosecution will make

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:40.620
an opening statement and they will explain

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:43.270
what's going to happen during
the course of the trial,

00:08:43.270 --> 00:08:46.800
it's generally not the time for argument.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:51.170
Then there will be a recess
and the defense will be able to

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:52.340
make their opening statement.

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:56.800
So it is very problematic
that here the defense

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:58.770
is not allowed to make any
statement what so ever.

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:02.080
Now I don't know what opening
statement you could possibly

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:04.520
make when you have no
idea what the facts are,

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:06.410
you haven't spoken to you client

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:09.890
and you have no idea what
arguments are going to be made

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:12.530
and what's good for your
client and what's not

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:14.720
but still, he needs to be afforded

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:17.460
an opportunity to explain his case.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:20.218
- [Payne] We also found a
photograph taken on this trip.

00:09:20.218 --> 00:09:21.380
- [Larry] Huh?

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:23.430
That's the thinker.

00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:25.630
- [Payne] Your honor I
would also like to submit

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:27.443
these items as evidence.

00:09:27.443 --> 00:09:28.420
- [Judge] I'll accept them.

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:32.230
- Okay so the prosecutor
here has not laid any kind

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:36.510
of foundation to allow these
exhibits into evidence,

00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:39.190
he's just simply said that
here's this photograph

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:41.310
and here's this passport,

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:45.130
he hasn't established that
those pieces of documentary

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:46.740
evidence are what they pro port to be

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:50.010
and he certainly hasn't
established through this witness

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:51.220
that the foundation has been laid.

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:53.450
You have to establish a foundation

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:56.350
before you're allowed
to bring evidence in.

00:09:56.350 --> 00:09:59.260
But there's a more
fundamental problem here,

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:01.740
the first witness that
the prosecution has called

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:03.570
is the criminal defendant.

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:05.770
Now in the US at least criminal defendants

00:10:05.770 --> 00:10:07.620
have a right against self incrimination

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:10.930
so they don't have to
testify if they don't want to

00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:13.360
now if they do testify then the prosecutor

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:15.410
will get an opportunity to cross examine.

00:10:15.410 --> 00:10:18.930
But it's crazy that the
first witness the prosecution

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:21.030
has called is the defendant

00:10:21.030 --> 00:10:23.820
and the defense hasn't objected,

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:25.963
they have allowed this to go forward,

00:10:27.878 --> 00:10:29.830
they are just asleep at
the wheel at this point

00:10:29.830 --> 00:10:32.523
and this is really, really bad lawyering.

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:36.410
- Mr. Sahwit to the best
of your recollection

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:39.330
please tell the court what
you saw the day of the murder.

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:42.120
- Okay why is the judge
asking questions here?

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:46.020
Now sometimes a judge will
ask follow up questions,

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:48.500
they have a certain amount
of leeway in the court,

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:50.950
it's their court, they can
sort of do what they want.

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:53.410
But to start off the
examination it should be

00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:55.710
the prosecutor, he should
be asking the questions,

00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:56.990
laying the foundation

00:10:56.990 --> 00:10:59.480
and establishing the
narrative of what happened,

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:00.660
it's improper for the judge

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:03.970
to be doing that kind
of in depth questioning.

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:05.745
- Why use a pay phone?

00:11:05.745 --> 00:11:07.870
Was there not one closer to the scene?

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:10.820
- Yes but unfortunately due
to some utility repairs there

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:14.010
was a scheduled black out
in the area from one to six.

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:15.313
So he couldn't use it.

00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:19.500
- The phone would be
completely inoperable.

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:20.333
- A black out?

00:11:20.333 --> 00:11:24.450
- Alright again, the prosecutor
is not allowed to answer

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:26.860
questions in an examination

00:11:26.860 --> 00:11:30.280
and the prosecutor can't
just magically pull out

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:32.470
some record and enter it into evidence,

00:11:32.470 --> 00:11:35.280
especially in the middle of an examination

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:38.350
of a witness that has nothing
to do with that information.

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:40.080
This is very wacky.

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:41.960
- There you have it, the motive, weapon,

00:11:41.960 --> 00:11:44.587
and a witness placing Butz at the scene.

00:11:44.587 --> 00:11:46.410
(chuckles)

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:47.250
- Okay.

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:48.587
- Is something wrong?

00:11:48.587 --> 00:11:51.030
- Is, is the prosecution resting here?

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:53.910
- Now Mr. Wright begin
your cross examination.

00:11:53.910 --> 00:11:56.100
- Oh okay, so the defense finally gets to

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:58.130
cross examine a witness here.

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:01.450
Right now there's so many
constitutional violations

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:04.560
that this would be declared
a mistrial like immediately.

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:08.530
This would be over turned on appeal within

00:12:08.530 --> 00:12:12.269
weeks of the verdict
coming down, this is crazy.

00:12:12.269 --> 00:12:14.330
(dramatic music)

00:12:14.330 --> 00:12:16.810
- You say you found the body at two p.m.,

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:18.920
are you absolutely certain of the time?

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:20.470
- [Sahwit] Yes without a doubt.

00:12:21.860 --> 00:12:24.760
- But according to the
autopsy report the victim died

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:26.390
sometime after four p.m.,

00:12:26.390 --> 00:12:28.233
it's right here in black and white,

00:12:29.530 --> 00:12:32.630
- Okay now while this might
seem like a good argument,

00:12:32.630 --> 00:12:37.630
you're contradicting the
witness with a coroners report.

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:40.660
You're really not allowed to
ask those kinds of questions

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:42.630
of this witness, this is a lay witness

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:44.470
who was there at the scene

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:47.780
but this witness is not
a coroner, not a doctor

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:50.700
and was not involved in
the autopsy of the victim

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:55.460
so he was no knowledge
about the autopsy report

00:12:55.460 --> 00:12:58.290
and so whatever testimony he might have

00:12:58.290 --> 00:13:01.490
about the autopsy report
is totally useless.

00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:03.480
The only time that you
would want to bring up

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:06.530
the autopsy report is if
you called the coroner

00:13:06.530 --> 00:13:09.190
up to the stand, which you
should in this particular case,

00:13:09.190 --> 00:13:12.000
and what you would wanna do
is verify that the coroner

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:15.070
was absolutely certain
about the time of death.

00:13:15.070 --> 00:13:17.940
Examination is not the time for argument.

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:20.860
The time of argument is
in your closing arguments.

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:22.930
- It's simple really, all we have to do is

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:25.600
cause the clock to sound,
right here and now.

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:26.516
- Yeah.

00:13:26.516 --> 00:13:28.100
(dramatic chord)

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:31.490
- Okay, she's apparently
offering testimony as well.

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:33.453
- [Clock] It's 9:25.

00:13:34.397 --> 00:13:35.670
- [Judge] That is certainly a bizarre way

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:36.740
to announce the time.

00:13:36.740 --> 00:13:38.227
- [Larry] It's totally awesome,

00:13:38.227 --> 00:13:39.726
I'm the voice of the thinker.

00:13:39.726 --> 00:13:40.559
(cries out)

00:13:40.559 --> 00:13:42.640
- [Judge] But I'm sorry to
say it doesn't prove anything

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:44.650
one way or another.

00:13:44.650 --> 00:13:48.620
- So this judge is making
a classic mistake between

00:13:48.620 --> 00:13:51.730
evidence being helpful, AKA probative

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:55.070
and being sufficient to
prove the entire case.

00:13:55.070 --> 00:13:58.840
No one can ever prove their
case with one piece of evidence.

00:13:58.840 --> 00:14:00.820
What your doing is you're
building a mountain

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:02.900
one stone at a time.

00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:05.980
Or you're trying to
create reasonable doubt

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:07.440
one stone at a time.

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:09.510
And after a while if
you have enough stones

00:14:09.510 --> 00:14:12.130
then you can have a mountain
of evidence in your favor.

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:15.220
Here the defense is
creating reasonable doubt

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:18.630
no thanks to their own
really terrible lawyering,

00:14:18.630 --> 00:14:21.630
one piece at a time through
every piece of evidence.

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:24.380
It really, really bugs
me when I see people make

00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:27.340
the same error that the
judge has here where they say

00:14:27.340 --> 00:14:29.910
oh, that piece of evidence
whether it's in a trial

00:14:29.910 --> 00:14:32.420
or in politics or what have you

00:14:32.420 --> 00:14:34.210
and they say well that doesn't prove

00:14:34.210 --> 00:14:35.570
what you're saying is true.

00:14:35.570 --> 00:14:38.860
No maybe it doesn't but it helps prove it

00:14:38.860 --> 00:14:42.190
and along with all this
mountain of evidence

00:14:42.190 --> 00:14:43.740
that does prove the case

00:14:43.740 --> 00:14:46.630
well you have to look at
everything weaved together.

00:14:46.630 --> 00:14:48.350
The judge is not doing that here.

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:50.670
- But it doesn't matter one
bit if the clock is running

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:52.910
slow today, because you can't prove that

00:14:52.910 --> 00:14:55.700
it was running slow on
the day of the murder.

00:14:55.700 --> 00:14:59.490
- Again so this witness, in
addition to answering a question

00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:02.720
that wasn't asked and the
attorney making an argument

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:04.410
in the middle of an examination

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:07.700
is make the same error that the judge is.

00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:10.380
He's saying well just because
you've shown something

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:12.710
that's very damaging that
doesn't go all the way,

00:15:12.710 --> 00:15:14.980
you know you've advanced 20 yards

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:17.950
but you haven't actually
entered the end zone.

00:15:17.950 --> 00:15:19.710
He's saying well it's now worthless.

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:23.090
That's not true, you
made forward progress,

00:15:23.090 --> 00:15:25.080
you might not have gotten all the way

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:26.380
but you're getting there

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:28.660
and you're putting all
the pieces together.

00:15:28.660 --> 00:15:30.870
And together they've probably already

00:15:30.870 --> 00:15:32.363
established reasonable doubt.

00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:35.510
- Well got an answer for me?

00:15:35.510 --> 00:15:37.380
Take that smart guy.

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:40.330
- Well as the attorney
he doesn't have to answer

00:15:40.330 --> 00:15:41.930
any questions that you ask him.

00:15:41.930 --> 00:15:44.630
- You got nothing on me pal.

00:15:44.630 --> 00:15:47.210
- No he's got lots against you.

00:15:47.210 --> 00:15:51.160
But also the defense attorney
here isn't trying to show

00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:53.840
that the witness in particular
committed the murder,

00:15:53.840 --> 00:15:55.640
all he has to do is show that there's

00:15:55.640 --> 00:15:58.130
a reasonable doubt that his client did.

00:15:58.130 --> 00:16:01.650
There's a big difference,
that's often why defendants

00:16:01.650 --> 00:16:03.260
actually don't take the stand,

00:16:03.260 --> 00:16:05.790
because it's not their burden to show

00:16:05.790 --> 00:16:07.150
that they are innocent.

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:09.160
All you have to do is
show that the prosecution

00:16:09.160 --> 00:16:13.540
hasn't met their burden or that
there is a reasonable doubt

00:16:13.540 --> 00:16:16.750
associated with the prosecutors case.

00:16:16.750 --> 00:16:19.300
The defense and especially
in a criminal case

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:22.060
bears no burden what so ever.

00:16:22.060 --> 00:16:24.680
- If you have nothing further
I have no other option

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:27.190
than to release Mr. Sahwit from the stand.

00:16:27.190 --> 00:16:29.290
- I take time out of my
day to relive this horrible

00:16:29.290 --> 00:16:31.260
experience and you call me a murderer?

00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:34.150
You might want to reconsider
your career Mr. Wright.

00:16:34.150 --> 00:16:37.580
- And as with defendants if
there's no question pending

00:16:37.580 --> 00:16:39.400
the witness should not be speaking.

00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:42.780
And also if you need time
to look into something,

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:46.080
or to recoup then you
would just ask the judge

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:49.250
for a short recess, that's very common.

00:16:49.250 --> 00:16:51.910
So you're gonna have time
to put things together,

00:16:51.910 --> 00:16:55.493
it's not going at the break
neck pace of this anime.

00:16:56.420 --> 00:16:58.150
- According to her passport

00:16:58.150 --> 00:17:00.550
the victim had just been over seas,

00:17:00.550 --> 00:17:04.160
the time difference between
Japan and New York is 14 hours.

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:07.190
So when it's four p.m.
here, it's two a.m. there.

00:17:07.190 --> 00:17:08.257
Two is four.

00:17:08.257 --> 00:17:10.043
- That's two hours difference.

00:17:11.930 --> 00:17:13.900
- So this is not something
you would wanna bring up

00:17:13.900 --> 00:17:18.500
with this particular witness
but there is a vehicle

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:21.400
to bring in evidence that is
basically incontrovertible

00:17:21.400 --> 00:17:23.050
and it is called judicial notice.

00:17:23.050 --> 00:17:26.440
If you have evidence of
something that you don't want

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:27.990
to bring in a particular witness,

00:17:27.990 --> 00:17:30.330
for example if you wanted
to say the time difference

00:17:30.330 --> 00:17:32.840
between New York and Tokyo is 14 hours,

00:17:32.840 --> 00:17:34.400
you wouldn't want to have to go through

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:37.660
the expense of calling in
an expert in date and time.

00:17:37.660 --> 00:17:40.240
It's a fact that's so incontrovertible

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:44.450
and easy to ascertain that the
judge should allow that fact

00:17:44.450 --> 00:17:47.050
in without requiring someone to lay

00:17:47.050 --> 00:17:49.290
what would normally be proper foundation.

00:17:49.290 --> 00:17:53.060
So here though he should
not be making an argument

00:17:53.060 --> 00:17:56.560
he would be able to ask the
judge to take judicial notice

00:17:56.560 --> 00:17:59.780
of the fact that the time
difference is 14 hours,

00:17:59.780 --> 00:18:03.163
that's something that would
come in virtually at anytime.

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:06.840
- The victim took the
clock with her on her trip

00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:09.670
but she hadn't set it to
reflect local time yet.

00:18:09.670 --> 00:18:10.621
- Well that's speculation.

00:18:10.621 --> 00:18:15.100
- That's why the time you heard
was exactly two hours off.

00:18:15.100 --> 00:18:17.248
The truth is Sahwit did it!

00:18:17.248 --> 00:18:18.390
(crying out)

00:18:18.390 --> 00:18:22.030
- Man I really wish I was
allowed to bring in evidence

00:18:22.030 --> 00:18:23.250
when ever I felt like it

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:25.540
and make argument when ever I felt like it

00:18:25.540 --> 00:18:27.030
but there are lots of laws

00:18:27.030 --> 00:18:29.260
and rules that prevent me from doing that.

00:18:29.260 --> 00:18:32.848
But with your help we can change that.

00:18:32.848 --> 00:18:34.184
(cries out)
(thudding)

00:18:34.184 --> 00:18:36.110
(banging)

00:18:36.110 --> 00:18:38.113
- Arrest Mr Sahwit immediately.

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:41.620
- The judge is not responsible
for arresting people.

00:18:42.620 --> 00:18:45.630
And in fact if the prosecutor
had seen this kind of evidence

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:49.220
he probably would have referred
the witness to the police

00:18:49.220 --> 00:18:51.900
to arrest that man but that is not

00:18:51.900 --> 00:18:55.000
the pre view of the judge in any case.

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:57.720
- I must say you've done
an incredible job today.

00:18:57.720 --> 00:19:00.080
In your first trial you
defended your client

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:01.650
and discovered the true culprit.

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:03.279
- Thank you.

00:19:03.279 --> 00:19:04.112
- Did he?

00:19:04.112 --> 00:19:06.255
- And now it's time to
deliver the verdict.

00:19:06.255 --> 00:19:08.340
(dramatic music)

00:19:08.340 --> 00:19:09.226
Not guilty.

00:19:09.226 --> 00:19:10.330
(cheering)

00:19:10.330 --> 00:19:13.010
- Well there shouldn't
be a verdict at all,

00:19:13.010 --> 00:19:14.500
the case just should have been dismissed.

00:19:14.500 --> 00:19:16.550
The prosecutor should have realized that

00:19:16.550 --> 00:19:18.950
it's crazy to continue
prosecuting this guy

00:19:18.950 --> 00:19:21.320
so therefore he should just go home.

00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:22.543
- [Man] All rise.

00:19:23.770 --> 00:19:27.530
- So that was Phoenix
Wright, Ace Attorney.

00:19:27.530 --> 00:19:29.840
Man I don't even know
what to do with that.

00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:33.750
I don't know if that's accurate
from a Japanese perspective

00:19:33.750 --> 00:19:35.550
but man from an American perspective

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:38.060
that is just all completely wrong.

00:19:38.060 --> 00:19:41.400
The US constitution gives
the benefit of the doubt

00:19:41.400 --> 00:19:44.440
to criminal defendants because
the founders recognized

00:19:44.440 --> 00:19:47.530
that it was better for
a guilty man to go free

00:19:47.530 --> 00:19:49.100
than an innocent man to be convicted

00:19:49.100 --> 00:19:50.810
of a crime they didn't commit.

00:19:50.810 --> 00:19:53.260
All of those things were
a due process violation

00:19:53.260 --> 00:19:56.180
that probably would have
been overturned on appeal.

00:19:56.180 --> 00:19:58.550
But it never should have
gotten to that point,

00:19:58.550 --> 00:20:01.860
and frankly the lawyering on
the defense side was terrible,

00:20:01.860 --> 00:20:03.770
he wasn't objecting when
he should have been,

00:20:03.770 --> 00:20:06.440
he was allowing the other
side to make argument

00:20:06.440 --> 00:20:07.630
when they shouldn't have been.

00:20:07.630 --> 00:20:11.040
So all in all I would give
Phoenix Wright, Ace Attorney

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:13.590
a D-, it is all over the place,

00:20:13.590 --> 00:20:15.610
it's all completely wrong

00:20:15.610 --> 00:20:18.890
and it's a terrible example
of lawyering in general.

